35,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"It appears that our previous projects have been most successful when the resulting spaces are open for general booking, whereas those limited to a single department have underperformed.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Victoria,,"That is an interesting point, Toshio. I found that every room taken out of service for works required expensive off-site hired space, which suggests we must be very careful about the disruption caused.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,George,,"My findings show that while front-of-house and grounds posts remain unfilled, our technicians actually have spare capacity, though they are spending significant time on manual checks that equipment should handle.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Mizuki,,"I've discovered that projects based on comfort or enrollment appeal often slip by a year or more, and older blocks frequently contain hazardous materials that cause significant delays.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"My research indicates that mechanical and ducted plant costs have risen by about a fifth, while we have failed to establish any renewal reserve for replacing older audiovisual equipment.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"@George, it sounds like the technicians are burdened by outdated equipment. If we can automate those manual routines, we might alleviate some of that pressure.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Mizuki, given the delays you found in older blocks and the costs I saw for off-site space, we should prioritize projects that minimize structural disruption to existing facilities.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,George,,"@Victoria, I agree. However, we must also consider that the current manual burden on our staff is unsustainable; any plan should ideally reduce the daily grind for our technicians.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@Benjamin, considering the rise in mechanical costs you mentioned, do you think a large-scale ventilation or plant upgrade would be financially risky given our history of budget slips?","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"It is a valid concern, Mizuki. Given the twenty percent increase in plant costs and our lack of renewal reserves, a heavy mechanical project could easily exceed its budget if not managed with extreme precision.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"@Victoria, if we prioritize general-use spaces as my data suggests, we might find a project that balances high utility with the operational constraints you've highlighted.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Victoria,,"@Toshio, that seems a logical path. If we can find a project that serves a wide user base without requiring extensive room closures or hazardous material removal, it would be ideal.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,George,,"I would be inclined to support any plan that specifically addresses the technicians' manual burdens, as that is a tangible way to support our remaining staff.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Mizuki,,"@George, I understand the need to support staff, but we must be cautious. If the project involves older blocks, we risk the same hazardous material delays I discovered, which would only add to the stress.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,It seems we are caught between the necessity of upgrading failing systems and the risk of budget and schedule overruns. We must weigh the cost of inaction against the probability of a financial shortfall.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Toshio,,I believe we should look for a candidate that maximizes accessibility for the whole campus while avoiding the pitfalls of departmental silos and excessive structural risk.,"{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Victoria,,I agree with Toshio. We need a solution that provides broad utility but avoids the high costs of off-site space rentals and the unpredictability of older building interiors.,"{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,George,,"I still believe we cannot ignore the technicians' plight; if a candidate exists that automates their manual routines, it should be our priority regardless of the general utility.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Mizuki,,"I agree that staff welfare is vital, but we must ensure the project is actually deliverable within the fiscal year. A plan that slips by twelve months helps no one.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"We have a delicate balance to strike between operational necessity, staff welfare, and fiscal realism. Let us see which candidates align with these constraints before we cast our votes.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
